<section id="story" class="px-8 sm:px-14 py-[160px] relative z-10"
         style="background: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent 0%, var(--color-cream-soft) 40%, transparent 100%);">
  <div class="max-w-[920px] mx-auto text-center">
    <div class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.18em] opacity-55 uppercase mb-9 flex items-center justify-center gap-3.5">
      <span class="block w-7 h-px bg-ink opacity-40"></span>
      03 — Story · a marginal essay
      <span class="block w-7 h-px bg-ink opacity-40"></span>
    </div>

    <p class="font-serif font-light italic m-0 leading-[1.22] text-balance"
       style="font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 48px); color: var(--color-ink-soft);">
      <span class="text-accent" style="font-size: 1.4em; line-height: 0; vertical-align: -0.18em; margin-right: 4px;">“</span>
      I keep walking into the same question from different rooms.
      The neuroscientist asks: what is this brain actually doing?
      The Buddhist asks: what is attention, and who is paying it?
      The engineer asks: could a machine ever know it's thinking?
      Three traditions,
      <span class="text-accent">one question</span> —
      what is a mind, and what is it like from the inside?
      The photographs, the cities, the half-finished essays — those are just me,
      paying closer attention to the answer.
      <span class="text-accent" style="font-size: 1.4em; line-height: 0; vertical-align: -0.18em; margin-left: 4px;">”</span>
    </p>

  </div>
</section>
